The Human-English defines \(x \sqsubseteq y\) on a \(T_0\)-space by the condition that every open set containing \(x\) contains \(y\), records that the relation is a preorder, and says that antisymmetry is exactly equivalent to the \(T_0\) axiom. Conversely, every poset arises by taking as open sets the upper sets, and \(\{x \mid x \not\sqsubseteq y\}\) is then open, so the space is \(T_0\); imposing also that a directed set whose supremum lies in \(U\) must already meet \(U\) gives the induced topology, in which those same complements of principal down-sets remain open, and a directed set, regarded as a net, converges to its supremum. A monotone net is a map from a directed index set into a \(T_0\)-space with \(i \le j\) implying \(x_i \sqsubseteq x_j\); such a net converges to each of its terms, it is constant in a \(T_1\)-space, and if it converges to an upper bound then that bound is the least one, because an open neighbourhood of the bound contains some term and the specialization order then puts every upper bound of that term into the neighbourhood. `SpecializationLe` is the open-set definition, `specializationPreorder` proves reflexivity by persistence of membership and transitivity through the intermediate point, and `specializationLe_iff_specializes` records that this order is the reverse of Mathlib’s specializes relation, since \(y \rightsquigarrow x\) is the condition that every open containing \(x\) contains \(y\). `specializationLe_antisymm` uses that reversal: the two specialization hypotheses are the two specializes directions, hence inseparability, hence equality in a \(T_0\)-space; the converse, from antisymmetry of specialization to \(T_0\), is not proved. `ScottOpen` is an upper set inaccessible by suprema of nonempty directed sets, which is the pair of conditions defining the induced topology, and `ScottOpen_iff_dirSupInacc`, `isOpen_iff_scottOpen`, `isOpen_scott_iff_scottOpen`, and `scottTopologicalSpace` identify the opens of the Scott topology of the universe with those sets. `scottOpen_univ`, `scottOpen_inter`, and `scottOpen_sUnion` check that these sets form a topology, the intersection step using directedness to find a common upper bound of the two witnesses. `scottOpen_not_le` shows that \(\{z \mid z \not\le L\}\) is Scott-open, by the upper-set argument and because a supremum of elements lying below \(L\) still lies below \(L\), and `specializationLe_scott_iff` uses that set to prove that specialization in the Scott topology is the lattice order. `scottTopology_t0Space` turns inseparability into the two lattice inequalities via that characterization. `MonotoneNet` only requires a monotone map from a directed preorder into a complete lattice, and `ScottConvergesTo` only requires that every Scott-open neighbourhood of the limit contain a tail; neither is stated for an arbitrary \(T_0\)-space, and neither proves convergence of a monotone net to its terms, the least-upper-bound argument, constancy in a \(T_1\)-space, or convergence of a directed set to its supremum. `SpecializationLeu2emap` adds that a continuous map preserves specialization, which this note does not claim. The upper-set topology without directed-supremum inaccessibility is not defined. **Theorem:** partial — the specialization preorder, the induced topology as Scott-open sets, recovery of the lattice order, and \(T_0\) of the Scott topology are present; antisymmetry is only derived from \(T_0\), and the net and convergence claims are not proved. **Proof:** partial — reflexivity, transitivity, the Scott-open criteria, and separation by \(\{z \mid z \not\le y\}\) match the text, while the written argument that a monotone net converging to an upper bound is the least upper bound has no counterpart.
